#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198047 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/id #uuid "fb947756-3471-59c8-b3c4-9b21a1a91557" :question/math-academy-id "e-12457" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + y$ given that $2x^2 + 3y^2 \\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= 2x^2 + 3y^2  = 1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  = 1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+3{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda = \\dfrac12$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y = \\dfrac{1}{6} \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y = \\dfrac1{3}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right), \\qquad  \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left(0, \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(0, -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $-\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $\\dfrac23.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "935e8644-8d66-5be6-87ec-b8176675aa91" :question/math-academy-id "e-4233" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 23 = \\dfrac 2x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 23 = \\dfrac 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 32 = \\dfrac x2\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n![](images/8e/8e7b2d027a761ba93f679cfb517709811938da412b06e59212391741b5660a2b.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "e7d11d09-773c-58a9-8582-2cd47a3b141c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4234" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x+1}{1} = \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\dfrac 12 .\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n![](images/d7/d7150c6093121f9f6dc0ecd9675d5be8f0855d339f3404d7a357875362d9990f.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "3ae175a8-7654-57c8-b5d8-33d7892b7a24" :question/math-academy-id "e-4235" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x-2}{1} = \\dfrac{5x}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to \n\n$$\nx-2 = \\dfrac{5x}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (x-2) & =3\\cdot \\frac{5x}{3} \\\\ 3x-6 & =5x \\\\ 3x-5x & =6 \\\\ -2x & =6 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "13f9fd01-802e-5331-b422-b9aa2cdda7ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-4236" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=\\dfrac 12$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n\n- $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{2x-1}{1} = \\dfrac{x+1}1\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n2x-1 = x+1.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =x+1 \\\\ 2x-x & =1+1 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228954 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229140 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "d7e995ec-7c1a-510d-be52-5ef3ce5f77aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-6618" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny'' - 2y' + y = 18x e^{x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x} + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-18\\int {x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =18\\int x\\,dx \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-6{x}^{3}){e}^{x}+(9{x}^{2})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3}{e}^{x}+9{x}^{3}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+\\underbrace{3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166654 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74510348-04f1-570b-b3f7-5fcb1ed1beef" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "fba5b411-86fe-5d2f-85ee-87cfa1e96d83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8465" :question/problem "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(4)$, compute $P(X = 2)$, rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(4)$, so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{4^x e^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 2) &= f(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4^{2}e^{-4}}{2!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1465\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30bc81e6-8fff-54e2-ac15-49e3fb530116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503070} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff51bff-7de4-5253-9d95-cedff81705fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503245} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0aa041-1831-5a03-87fe-4c02c07d7e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503266} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dc91f3-4ee3-57a4-8547-157373bab8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503304} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fd723a-f248-5a39-96ba-76e1a1a6935a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff51bff-7de4-5253-9d95-cedff81705fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503245} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c930558-3f13-59ed-984d-aa81dfac2ac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e3eaae0-125c-585a-90f2-bbc8b4da35cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-106354" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x+5}=\\frac{3}{2x+12}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{1}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+15}{3}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x+15}{3}\\right) & =3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2x+12}{3}\\right) \\\\ 3x+15 & =2x+12 \\\\ 3x-2x & =12-15 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26df7659-281a-55ad-9717-8a068ed8d410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446748} {:answer/id #uuid "29ba9e62-696e-5df4-a785-8720b21f1d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446750} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fc2520-2872-5f53-b5da-91567e75c405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446782} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3270e4-ba18-50fc-871d-abe0c18290ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446786} {:answer/id #uuid "e475e7cd-dfed-5fbd-ae10-5b0c7895d481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2fc2520-2872-5f53-b5da-91567e75c405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446782} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ab2ac1e-1760-5933-be14-b547164eb087" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6e0abbd-abc7-5917-a015-ff462bc321f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162781" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=4\\lambda x \\\\ -4 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}y=-2\\,⇒\\,y=-4.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+(-4)^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(0,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-8$ | $8$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1715e06f-e211-5b5b-b085-4dce3fe10cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278797} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae4dbb2-6216-51cc-9650-90dd9779ab6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279260} {:answer/id #uuid "b44db878-4ead-5dad-a363-920768dc38d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280179} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fb5f2f-a80f-5006-a9c4-be4750a711f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280294} {:answer/id #uuid "e19f9127-76af-507a-8b89-9b102194e39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b44db878-4ead-5dad-a363-920768dc38d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280179} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9a1c281-1dcb-592f-8bb1-786e3c4e7bdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b349e078-f370-57a4-8287-923743beec39" :question/math-academy-id "q-162790" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=x-{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda x &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y(1+\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 2,0),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,0),$ and its maximum value is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229140 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f063173-e6fe-5d2a-99b7-378065d2b1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{3x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308984} {:answer/id #uuid "40cef204-8013-5a17-b8d9-ae936e8062e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{5}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309242} {:answer/id #uuid "6700055d-6c65-5ea7-bc2b-4856d3527804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309538} {:answer/id #uuid "67a87526-29fc-5bf6-8e12-907f098b41ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309542} {:answer/id #uuid "8a51098b-f1b1-5736-8996-e4133727d23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{3}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6700055d-6c65-5ea7-bc2b-4856d3527804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309538} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b23d5d-5883-57bd-8005-c893ed0c9ce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0163f286-fc30-51ed-907e-500011ffd762" :question/math-academy-id "q-337541" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-12y=14x{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=14x{e}^{-4x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{-4x})^{′}({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(3{e}^{3x})-(-4{e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}+4{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =7{e}^{-x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{-4x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2})({e}^{-4x})+[-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}]({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(A-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-\\frac{2}{49})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}\\underbrace{-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89e8227a-a21e-51d1-92e0-947e1e75d9ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197547} {:answer/id #uuid "9e490a88-3cfe-518e-a669-c4bca5084f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197548} {:answer/id #uuid "aaeef0c9-8497-5a8b-8705-e583cc0896c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197549} {:answer/id #uuid "f081f56f-4d9d-51a0-aa93-86bf9926b018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197550} {:answer/id #uuid "fae80050-937d-51f5-858f-521dcefb84ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e490a88-3cfe-518e-a669-c4bca5084f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197548} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f61eef3-fecf-50ec-985f-6e7b0490b34c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad6a612f-7b80-5294-8d9b-5ae88e52a2b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-37781" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,-2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=9\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 3).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}y=-1\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(-2)^{2}=9\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 3),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(0,-3)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $6$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,3),$ and its minimum value is $-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a437b9ff-ebf6-5b44-bf7f-be36e82b3f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058843} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c792e2-a542-5e9b-a88b-cedeb1c0150e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058844} {:answer/id #uuid "b746e675-67cc-5f10-9cfb-c66b3279686b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058845} {:answer/id #uuid "cbccd66e-1635-58c5-97c9-56ffb4fd0b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058846} {:answer/id #uuid "021479db-7ec1-5c8a-bdb8-49cdc6d17874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058847}] :answer-field/id #uuid "78bcad99-fb77-58a5-a3c3-0330563127da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058842}] :question/id #uuid "eb4b681b-2ab2-5879-bf11-cb5badc0bb00" :question/math-academy-id "q-67308" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{x} = \\frac{1}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c71a3a11-3eba-5020-9597-3de35a5ab785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058867} {:answer/id #uuid "c768ab08-49e9-5e5c-928d-98edae762f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058868} {:answer/id #uuid "cf329fcc-fb6f-59d1-adcc-57bc40719b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{42}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058869} {:answer/id #uuid "22218b3e-4939-5119-bb7f-f0028c856a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058870} {:answer/id #uuid "2c81b1e5-f63c-5724-81ac-8400764b9ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22218b3e-4939-5119-bb7f-f0028c856a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058870} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfde7113-aa52-5603-817f-a4c288821339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b4e7858-1264-5def-86e5-7598a8adf5a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-67329" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x-3}=\\frac{2}{x+6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{x+6}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $6.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{3x+18}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{3x+18}{6} \\\\ x-3 & =3x+18 \\\\ x-3x & =18+3 \\\\ -2x & =21 \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(-2x) & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)21 \\\\ (-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})(-\\cancel{2}x) & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)21 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9afe93af-c815-5f2c-ac75-9a584d561ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058879} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d50060-ed5a-55ba-98a5-e136d622f6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058880} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d7edad-f24a-5a39-b3a9-bdf0e222d37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058881} {:answer/id #uuid "3af8e372-8b94-5bca-966e-b13590d4c251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058882} {:answer/id #uuid "4335e387-f8cb-50d1-a3a5-f1a739c9876b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3af8e372-8b94-5bca-966e-b13590d4c251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058882} :answer-field/id #uuid "084ec80c-c0ed-58a4-b621-614f03d00659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09320d7a-d867-531b-9740-66504912149d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67332" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3x+2}=-\\frac{4}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{2}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x+4}{4}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{6x+4}{4} & =4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{4}\\right) \\\\ 6x+4 & =-x \\\\ 6x+x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8be19f3-d1d1-5874-a267-0e4a8dae558d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058885} {:answer/id #uuid "df043132-a831-5ea4-b43a-b3fdb0795ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058886} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d6978c-429a-5678-9673-1dad8790f86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058887} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d08e45-da77-54d2-b0fb-f110b3c9d317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058888} {:answer/id #uuid "1a59f56e-4565-51e2-8e93-bdc2b248a059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a59f56e-4565-51e2-8e93-bdc2b248a059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058889} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4328795-e359-574d-9185-453cc4f416b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bbc3969-7e62-5072-9fb8-19f0fe79bb27" :question/math-academy-id "q-67355" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{1}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{x+1}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}=x+1\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & =x+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62c728d8-29c1-5f90-a9bc-17eae1a900d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503171} {:answer/id #uuid "7733ee4f-eef5-5fd6-a757-f34e447b3828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503219} {:answer/id #uuid "80d7c6b7-28c9-5bea-bee5-7bb87f769d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503246} {:answer/id #uuid "c044e13e-665f-594c-8087-19c0c493d990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503366} {:answer/id #uuid "da440c13-f203-5583-aa96-40df058f74cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62c728d8-29c1-5f90-a9bc-17eae1a900d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503171} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1354d1c-de34-5200-8c22-d682a6a448d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9423a212-cd5b-5be8-a581-5c447b673232" :question/math-academy-id "q-67407" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{4}{3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{3x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & =\\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} & =4\\cdot \\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{4}} & =\\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\cancel{4}} \\\\ 5 & =3x \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot 5 & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot (3x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot (\\cancel{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=\\frac{5}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d3d307-80c5-5100-8287-638a2bdecc60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502991} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd1f877-23e8-59f2-a2aa-0744c5e7dd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503013} {:answer/id #uuid "33f90d44-4628-5b5e-b9c2-d33e90471828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503075} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef03d2c-7e33-54bd-ac16-3e50ecf310c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503166} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd6e3e2-7e5b-5a15-8a25-1e78f2e97115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebd6e3e2-7e5b-5a15-8a25-1e78f2e97115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503463} :answer-field/id #uuid "44ae6554-557c-5337-b681-7691c583fdd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "528cc092-636b-5bd7-9846-07908de9113d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67416" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x-2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication and the addition principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x-2}{2}\\right) & =2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\ (\\cancel{2})\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{2}}\\right) & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-2+2 & =\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e09809f-1ac1-5163-95fe-56a6c3cca096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503015} {:answer/id #uuid "1702597c-5660-544c-a89c-cba9f00560d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503028} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7989a1-6ce6-581c-a185-11e1a6852423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503069} {:answer/id #uuid "78dcdf47-9629-5ab8-a15d-6bafe37b89e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503225} {:answer/id #uuid "94f3bb0d-e212-55c1-a64b-282f04cc5137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f7989a1-6ce6-581c-a185-11e1a6852423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503069} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4d1887c-0c5d-5ac5-9c0f-77c952d10182" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b01bcc83-5d54-554e-8eb4-318389e1d142" :question/math-academy-id "q-67422" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x+2}=\\frac{3}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{x}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $6.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{2x}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{2x}{6} \\\\ x+2 & =2x \\\\ 2 & =2x-x \\\\ 2 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55a6fcca-aad9-5ad1-a121-fdaf665d5aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503145} {:answer/id #uuid "d92f70d4-3067-5353-97b7-e64c25a54de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503415} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb302e9-c203-5981-98a6-1abb4868178a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503427} {:answer/id #uuid "e0db71b8-ad73-5cce-b817-b4e5c4f03d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503441} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb895ed-3b69-53d2-9ba7-f858762d5ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb302e9-c203-5981-98a6-1abb4868178a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503427} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbaaab81-294e-5a37-9fb1-b73c2d58a5c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a235d31-7002-5190-8de9-b78ca920ac5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-67425" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{3}{x}=\\frac{9}{3x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $9.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3x}{9}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x}{-9} & =(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x-1}{9} \\\\ 3x & =(-1)\\cdot (3x-1) \\\\ 3x & =1-3x \\\\ 3x+3x & =1-3x+3x \\\\ 6x & =1 \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\cdot 6x & =\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a3543e-a251-579f-b8fc-7f9ba24706ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571566} {:answer/id #uuid "1621b1c6-95e0-5b2c-bd45-64e852812aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571579} {:answer/id #uuid "65daad1e-0611-5bd7-afbf-4fd9443314b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571712} {:answer/id #uuid "adca5567-c435-5016-bb46-aa649ccb320e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571836} {:answer/id #uuid "fb02bb4e-edfe-536a-8159-eeaa09da9182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10a3543e-a251-579f-b8fc-7f9ba24706ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571566} :answer-field/id #uuid "b35c0d69-6d83-53fa-b5c9-4ab6a701cd32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50efbd3d-db78-53ec-922c-e4529ac97e50" :question/math-academy-id "q-67426" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x-5}=\\frac{2}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-5}{6}=\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $6.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-5}{6}=\\frac{3x}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x-5}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{3x}{6} \\\\ x-5 & =3x \\\\ x-x-5 & =3x-x \\\\ -5 & =2x \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (-5) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (2x) \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74510348-04f1-570b-b3f7-5fcb1ed1beef" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0357732-7d25-5b9d-85e1-5cab3acc7ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2691" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165867} {:answer/id #uuid "b8dae3a7-91fb-52c0-b0bc-cdbf28927294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2249" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165868} {:answer/id #uuid "0aeeb7c9-2abe-518f-b9b1-a59adb1fe821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165869} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2be036-5f5e-5179-889b-f45060731336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1547" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165870} {:answer/id #uuid "715aef0b-7e80-505a-90f3-c0bb91180806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1804" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165871}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8cbb5c90-610e-5a65-8619-e17d9a570b7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165866}] :question/id #uuid "4d4292bf-c73b-5439-8dac-84cef869be2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95993" :question/problem "Given $X∼Po(2)$, compute $P(X = 3)$, rounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c1fa27-09bb-576b-97dd-85dd401dc8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360976} {:answer/id #uuid "681a4a87-3b1d-58fd-b752-c86f6763e6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361305} {:answer/id #uuid "91c7e663-64a3-56ec-a432-8a3dc5cde46b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361480} {:answer/id #uuid "d174ba97-334a-5124-8075-96cc9ef5a6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361772} {:answer/id #uuid "dd38c98d-8f6b-5cf8-b355-869da372bc30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d174ba97-334a-5124-8075-96cc9ef5a6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361772} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce9a233-37ed-5628-bbcf-882c2c09d5d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85f60f32-4588-5316-bc63-974d85067877" :question/math-academy-id "q-99240" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=5-x+2y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1 &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{4\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n  Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5+3\\sqrt{2}$ | $5-3\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,,$ and its minimum value is $5-3\\sqrt{2}.$"}]}
